.TH THREAD_START 2
.SH NAME
thread_start, sys_thread_exit \- start and end threads (SYS_THREAD_CREATE, SYS_THREAD_EXIT)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long thread_start(void (*fn)(void *), void *arg, size_t stack);
void sys_thread_exit(void);

syscall4(SYS_THREAD_CREATE, entry, stack_top, arg, 0);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B SYS_THREAD_CREATE
starts a new thread in the calling process: it enters user mode at
.I entry
with
.I arg
as its first argument, on the stack whose top is
.I stack_top
(aligned down to 16 bytes, as if called). It shares the process's memory,
ports and identity, and may run at once on any processor.
.PP
The library's
.B thread_start
allocates
.I stack
bytes (plus the thread's copy of the thread-local variables, set with
.BR set_fs (2)),
and runs
.IR fn ( arg );
when
.I fn
returns, the thread ends.
.PP
.B sys_thread_exit
ends the calling thread. The process ends with its last thread, with
status 0; to end it at once with a status, see
.BR sys_exit (2).
A word registered with
.BR sys_thread_self (2)
is cleared and its futex waiter woken.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The new thread's id, or a negative error.
.B sys_thread_exit
does not return.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I entry
or
.I stack_top
is not a user address.
.TP
.B ENOMEM
No memory for the thread (or, in
.BR thread_start ,
for its stack).
.SH BUGS
A stack from
.B thread_start
is not freed when its thread ends.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sys_thread_self (2),
.BR set_fs (2),
.BR sys_futex (2),
.BR sys_place (2),
.BR sys_exit (2)
